    "image": "help/1768946365167.png",
    "html": "<p><strong>Workflow 1 — Targeted Reservoir Analysis</strong></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Goal:</strong> Quickly find everything ReSST knows about a <em>specific</em> dam, reservoir, or monitored reach—then review linked literature and export results. <strong>Use this workflow when:</strong> You already have a site in mind (by name, location, or known dam). <strong>What you’ll get:</strong> A site summary (key attributes) + the <strong>Site-linked Literature</strong> tied to that site, plus relevant <strong>General Literature</strong> you can compare against. <strong>Tip:</strong> Start broad (select the site), then narrow with filters only if needed. </p><p><br></p><p><strong>Step 1: Find the site (Search or map navigation)</strong></p><p>Use the <strong>Search</strong> box to type a reservoir/dam/site name (or a place name), <strong>OR</strong> Pan/zoom the <strong>Map</strong> to your area of interest. <strong>What to look for:</strong> The site point(s) on the map (dams/reservoirs/reaches). <strong>Result:</strong> You’re viewing the correct area and can select the site.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Step 2: Select the site on the map</strong></p><p>Click the site point on the <strong>Map</strong>. If multiple features are close together, select the correct one from the selection list (if prompted). <strong>Result:</strong> The site becomes the active selection, and the app updates the right panel and tables.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Step 3: Review the Selected Data panel (site context)</strong></p><p>In <strong>Selected Data</strong> (right panel), review the site’s key attributes (examples: Site Type, Sediment Release, Ecological Concern, Analysis). <strong>Why it matters:</strong> These attributes help you quickly determine whether this site is a useful analog for your project. <strong>Result:</strong> You understand the site’s basic context and “why it’s in the database.”</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Step 4: Open the Site Literature tab (linked references)</strong></p><p>In the <strong>Data Table</strong>, open the <strong>Site Literature</strong> tab. Scan titles/metadata and open links as needed. <strong>Tip:</strong> Site Literature is the fastest way to see what’s been documented <em>for that location</em>. <strong>Result:</strong> You’ve identified the references directly associated with the selected site.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Step 5: Compare with General Literature (broader methods + context)</strong></p><p>Switch to the <strong>General Literature</strong> tab. Look for methods, monitoring approaches, modeling types, or ecological considerations relevant to your site. <strong>Tip:</strong> General literature is not tied to one site—use it to broaden your reading list. <strong>Result:</strong> You’ve expanded from “site-specific” to “methods and themes.”</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Step 6: (Optional) Refine using Keyword Filters</strong></p><p>Open <strong>Keyword Filters</strong> (left panel). Toggle one or more filters (examples: Sediment Characteristic, Sediment Release mechanism, Ecological Concern, Modeling Methods). <strong>Best practice:</strong> Add filters one at a time so you can see what changes. <strong>Result:</strong> The map and tables update to only show matching records. </p><p><br></p><p><strong>Step 7: Export your results for offline review</strong></p><p>In the <strong>Data Table</strong>, export the currently filtered/selected records (Sites and/or Literature tabs). Save as Excel/CSV (or use in GIS) for comparison, tracking, or reporting. <strong>Result:</strong> You have a portable dataset and reading list for your project files.</p>"
